{
    "name": "Binary cubes",
    "views": [
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "toc",
            "items": [
                "Another abstract representation",
                "References"
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "Another abstract representation",
            "scrollToId": 0
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The simplicity in applying the absorption and logic adjacency in Karnaugh Maps is derived from another way of representing binary functions: the binary cubes."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "This representation is also key to understand the Quine McCluskey algorithm to minimize logic functions."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "More information can be found in the section on Karnaugh Maps in [1]"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H2",
            "content": "Functions of 1 variable"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Any logic function of a single variable $f(x)$ can be represented by a line:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "/assets/images/comb/binary-cube-1var.svg",
                "description": "Cube representing a logic function with one variable."
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The points at both ends of the line indicate the values that the variable $x$ can take (0 or 1)."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H2",
            "content": "Functions of 2 variables"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "In a similar way a function of two variables can be represented by a square in a two-dimensional space:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "/assets/images/comb/binary-cube-2var.svg",
                "description": "Cube representing a logic function with two variables."
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "In the figure it is easy to see that (1,0) and (0,1) are not adjacent, while (1,1) and (0,1) are adjacent."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H2",
            "content": "Functions of 3 variables and more"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "This representation can be further extended to higher orders. For instance, a function with three variables would be represented by the following three-dimensional cube:"
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "image",
            "content": {
                "link": "/assets/images/comb/binary-cube-3var.svg",
                "description": "Cube representing a logic function with three variables."
            }
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "Again, the adjacencies can be identified by the lines connecting the corners of the cube. If there is not a line, those binary numbers are not adjacent."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H3",
            "content": "The adjacency is key to group cubes in Karnaugh maps and in the Quine McCluskey method to obtain minimal expressions."
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "size": "H1",
            "content": "References",
            "scrollToId": 1
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "numbered_list",
            "items": [
                {
                    "content": "G. Donzellini, L. Oneto, D. Ponta, and D. Anguita, Introduction to Digital Systems Design. Springer International Publishing, 2018. Available: https://books.google.cl/books?id=va1qDwAAQBAJ"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "widget",
            "sub_type": "pop-quiz",
            "content": [
